<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/intermediate/urban-geography-and-cities">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cities are humanity's best invention for exchanging ideas — and its most expensive
    mistakes, sometimes on the same block. Around 2008, for the first time, more people
    lived in urban areas than rural ones, and nearly all the growth since has been urban:
    the UN expects about 68 per cent of humanity in cities by 2050, with almost every new
    urban resident added in Asian and African cities. Urban geography explains the whole
    machine: <strong>where cities grow, why land prices fall outward from the centre, how
    neighbourhoods sort themselves, what the opportunities and costs of that concentration
    are, and how growth can be managed</strong> — because the coming decades are less a
    question of whether people will crowd into cities than of what those cities will be
    built like.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four ideas, from von Thünen's rings to Jacobs' streets" widget="equationStep" config={urbanCfg} />

  <h2>Where people live: why cities exist where they do</h2>
  <p>
    Start with site and situation. <strong>Site</strong> is the ground itself — a river
    crossing, a sheltered harbour, fresh water, defensibility — which explains where the
    first towns rooted (London at the Thames' first bridging point, Rome on its Tiber
    island). <strong>Situation</strong> is a place's relation to everything else — the
    reason Singapore (nothing in the soil, everything on the sea lane between India and
    China) or Chicago (the rail junction between the grain of the plains and the Atlantic)
    dwarfed better-sited neighbours. But the deep reason cities keep growing is
    <strong>agglomeration</strong>: proximity cuts three costs at once. Firms <em>share</em>
    infrastructure and suppliers; employers and workers <em>match</em> with each other
    faster; and ideas <em>learn</em> by colliding — economists call the last one knowledge
    spillover. The result is a productivity premium: workers in denser cities earn more
    per hour than identical workers in small towns, which is exactly why people pay city
    rents. A quick bid-rent sketch: if a central site earns a shop 100 units above its
    costs and a site 5 km out only 60, the shop can bid up to 40 units more for the
    centre — and an apartment builder, whose location premium is smaller, loses every
    auction. Run that logic across every land user and the skyline draws itself.
    Cities also sort into hierarchies: in many countries the second city is about
    half the first, the third about a third (the rank-size rule), while other countries —
    France, Thailand, Mexico — concentrate almost everything in one <strong>primate
    city</strong> (Paris, Bangkok, Mexico City). Megacities (over 10 million) were three
    in 1975 and are thirty-plus now; Tokyo's region, at about 37 million, is larger than
    Canada.
  </p>
  <p>
    Populations also move through a characteristic urban cycle. <strong>Urbanisation</strong>
    (rural-to-urban migration plus natural growth) fills the city;
    <strong>suburbanisation</strong> pushes it out as cars and railways loosen the pull of
    the centre; <strong>counter-urbanisation</strong> leaks it to small towns and the
    countryside (the pandemic-era "zoom towns" were a spike on a long trend);
    <strong>re-urbanisation</strong> brings it back as deindustrialised docklands are
    rebuilt and the young return for jobs and street life — London's Docklands, from
    abandoned warehouses to Canary Wharf and apartments, is the canonical worked example.
    Each phase re-prices the land and re-sorts the residents, which is why "where people
    live" is a moving answer.
  </p>

  <h2>The layout: land values and the models</h2>
  <p>
    The organising variable of urban form is land price, and its logic is
    <strong>bid-rent</strong>: every user bids what the location earns them. A central
    shopfront earns more per square metre than anywhere else, so retail outbids everyone
    for the centre; offices outbid apartments; apartments outbid warehouses; warehouses
    outbid farming. Plot the winning bids against distance from the centre and you get a
    decaying curve — the reason skyscrapers cluster downtown, industry follows ring roads,
    and houses thin toward the edge. Von Thünen saw the same rings around an isolated town
    in 1826 (perishables and firewood near the market, extensive grazing far out) — the
    first model of the logic. Twentieth-century urban models refined it for the industrial
    city: <strong>Burgess</strong> (1925) drew concentric zones around a Chicago core —
    CBD, transition zone of looping immigration and decay, commuter rings;
    <strong>Hoyt</strong> (1939) bent the rings into <strong>sectors</strong> along tram
    lines and rail lines, which is why rich and poor corridors run as wedges, not rings;
    <strong>Harris and Ullman</strong> (1945) shattered the single centre into
    <strong>multiple nuclei</strong> — airports, industrial parks, edge-city malls — which
    is what car-era cities actually look like. Note the recurring asymmetry: in many
    low- and middle-income cities the highest-quality housing sits on the urban periphery's
    best land while informal settlements occupy hazard-prone land near the centre, the
    mirror image of the Chicago model, because land markets and housing shortages work
    differently when supply is informal.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> these models are "wrong" because no city matches them
    perfectly. They were never maps — they are theories of <em>why</em>, each isolating one
    force: transport cost (von Thünen), succession of immigrant neighbourhoods (Burgess),
    transport corridors (Hoyt), car ownership (Harris-Ullman). Real cities are all of them
    at once, plus history; judge a model by what it explains, not by what it omits.
  </p>

  <h2>Opportunities and challenges of urbanisation</h2>
  <p>
    The opportunities are why the migration happens. Cities pay more, treat more, educate
    more: they concentrate hospitals, universities, courts and jobs, and density makes
    services cheap to deliver (one bus route serves thousands). Urban dwellers, on average
    worldwide, have better access to water, electricity, schooling and healthcare than
    rural populations, and cities generate the majority of national GDP everywhere —
    productive concentration is the opportunity. The challenges are its shadow. First
    <strong>housing</strong>: demand outpaces formal supply in fast-growing cities, so over
    a billion people live in informal settlements — self-built districts like Mumbai's
    Dharavi, approaching a million people on roughly 2 km², simultaneously a deprived
    neighbourhood and an economic engine whose informal output is estimated near a billion
    dollars a year. Second <strong>infrastructure strain</strong>: water, sanitation, waste
    and power lag the population, and congestion eats the agglomeration gains (commuters in
    the largest megacities can lose weeks a year in traffic). Third <strong>environment and
    health</strong>: transport and industry concentrate air pollution — Delhi's PM2.5
    concentrations run many multiples of the WHO guideline in winter — and the urban heat
    island (concrete storing the day's heat) makes cities several degrees warmer than their
    countryside, which heatwaves turn lethal. Fourth <strong>inequality</strong>: gated
    Fourth <strong>inequality</strong>: gated
    towers beside informality make cities the most visible stage of the development gap
    (the inequality ladder connects directly). And a compounding geography of risk: informal
    settlements often occupy the land the formal city rejected — steep slopes, floodplains,
    drainable wetlands — so the residents with the least are exposed first to the floods,
    landslides and heat that growth itself intensifies.
  </p>

  <h2>Managing urban growth</h2>
  <p>
    The toolkit is real, and it works better in some places than others. <strong>Transit
    before cars</strong>: Curitiba, Brazil pioneered bus rapid transit in the 1970s —
    dedicated lanes, boarding tubes, feeder lines — moving metro-scale passenger volumes at
    a fraction of metro cost, and structuring its growth along the bus corridors.
    <strong>Build enough housing</strong>: Tokyo, with permissive national zoning and
    constant redevelopment, keeps a metro of 37 million affordable by global standards;
    San Francisco, with supply restricted, prices out everyone but the well-paid — same
    demand, opposite rules, opposite outcomes. <strong>Public housing at scale</strong>:
    Singapore's HDB boards house about 80 per cent of its residents in well-maintained,
    ethnically mixed estates, with most families owning — the strongest single proof that
    informality is a policy choice, not destiny. <strong>Plan the shape</strong>: green
    belts and new towns (London's post-war strategy), or China's alternative — Shenzhen,
    designated a Special Economic Zone in 1980 with roughly 30,000 people, now a city of
    over 17 million and a manufacturing-tech powerhouse: planned growth can work at
    staggering speed, though rarely without displacement and environmental cost.
    <strong>Upgrade, don't just clear</strong>: decades of evidence show evicting informal
    settlements moves poverty outward, while upgrading — water, sewers, electricity, paved
    lanes, secure tenure, resident participation — converts slums into neighbourhoods.
    <strong>Governance is infrastructure too</strong>: participatory budgeting, pioneered
    in Porto Alegre, Brazil, from 1989, let residents vote on capital spending and steered
    services toward the poorest districts. And the newest idea, <strong>proximity
    planning</strong>: Paris's "15-minute city"
    reorganises streets and services so daily needs sit within a short walk or ride —
    effectively Jacobs' street-level urbanism made into municipal strategy.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading a city: measurement and fieldwork</h2>
  <p>
    Urban geography has a practical toolkit for describing cities from evidence. Density is
    the base metric — but distinguish <strong>crude density</strong> (population divided by
    administrative area, which shrinks whenever a boundary expands) from
    <strong>built-up density</strong> (population per urbanised km²); Barcelona and Atlanta
    house similar populations on wildly different footprints, which is why their transport
    systems behave differently. Land use can be mapped and quantified directly: a student
    survey categorising buildings on a transect from the centre outward will usually
    rediscover bid-rent in its own data — shops thinning out, terraces becoming semis,
    gardens widening. Sphere of influence can be traced from where shoppers come; CBD
    boundaries identified by the shift from retail to residential frontage; deprivation
    indexed from census indicators (unemployment, crowding, car ownership). The transferable
    skill is exactly the maps lesson's: a city is a hypothesis about location, and any
    careful set of measurements will test it.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Two thinkers frame every modern argument. Jane Jacobs (<em>The Death and Life of Great
    American Cities</em>, 1961) watched actual streets and concluded that safety, vitality
    and innovation come from short blocks, mixed uses, aged buildings and "eyes on the
    street" — density at the <em>ground floor</em>, not the skyline. Edward Glaeser
    (<em>Triumph of the City</em>, 2011) quantified the same insight from data: cities are
    humanity's greatest invention because proximity makes us smarter and greener — a
    Manhattanite's carbon footprint is smaller than a suburbanite's. The synthesis is
    modern urbanism's operating principle: build dense, mixed, walkable, transit-served
    neighbourhoods and let street life do the rest. The unresolved frontier is spatial
    justice and lock-in: urban form lasts a century, so where highways, zoning and
    underinvestment sorted people by race and income mid-century, the damage compounds —
    and conversely, getting the next few decades of African and Asian urban growth right
    would be among the highest-leverage climate and development policies available. A
    closing measure of the stakes: the physical footprint of cities is under 3 per cent of
    land, but the majority of energy use, emissions, waste and innovation are urban. The
    city is the century's smallest geographies with its largest consequences.
  </p>
  <p>
    One closing correction to a persistent myth: cities are not "too big" by nature —
    they are the size their housing and transport allow them to be. Where supply is
    flexible and mobility cheap, big cities stay affordable and productive; where supply
    is rationed, the same growth becomes congestion and overcrowding. That is why
    "manage urban growth" always resolves into the same short list of levers — homes,
    transit, services, tenure, participation — pulled decades before the crisis, not
    after it.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Glaeser, Triumph of the City; Jacobs, The Death and Life of Great American Cities (1961); UN-Habitat World Cities Report; UN World Urbanization Prospects.</p>
